  "body": "Research umbrella: support Okuma machining centres with the **OSP control** (OSP-P300MA, P300MA-H, P500), starting with the **GENOS M560-V and M660-V** vertical mills.\n\n## Open questions\n1. **Dialect:** how OSP programs differ from Fanuc-style G-code. Program header and number, tool length offset (G56 H with OSP numbering), work offsets (G15 H, not G54), tool change (M06 T and the next-tool call), the program end, comments, units.\n2. **Cycles:** drilling, peck, tap and bore cycles in OSP form (G71 to G89 meanings that differ from Fanuc), and the arc format.\n3. **Coolant, through-spindle coolant and air blast M-codes; the spindle and high-speed modes (Super-NURBS or the HSM settings).**\n4. **Machine data** for machine files: travels, spindle range, the ATC, the max feeds and rapids, the table size.\n5. **What our Fanuc post already gets right,** and what an OSP post must change.\n\n## Sub-issues to file (when done)\n- `okuma-osp.cps` post with goldens.\n- Machine files: GENOS M560-V and M660-V.\n- A small test kit for a shop with an OSP control (air cut first).\n\n## Clean-room rules\n- **Used:** the machine maker's public documentation (programming and operator manuals, spec sheets, published application notes), public G-code standards, general machining knowledge, and code-usage statistics from programs that ran on a real machine and were made by the control itself, by hand, or by non-Autodesk CAM. Files are never committed; only word and code usage is summarised.\n- **Not used:** any Autodesk Fusion 360 or HSMWorks post or output (including the widely shared community posts for these machines, and NYC CNC's Fusion posts). Do not copy manual text: cite and summarise.\n\n## Why now\nmkinney, 2026-11-15: these machines are common in small professional shops, for example NYC CNC (Saunders Machine Works), which runs Tormach (supported), an Okuma GENOS M660-V (earlier an M560-V), Haas mills, and a Brother Speedio. Public sources: Okuma's Saunders Machine Works case study, the Saunders Machine Works careers page, Tormach's John Saunders pages.\n\n**Research, not code.** Pick never takes this issue. It is answered in comments, then broken into sub-issues (AGENTS.md \"needs-research\").\n\nDone means: each open question has an answered comment, the sub-issues are filed with \"Part of #this\", and this umbrella is closed.\n",
